<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Marija Gimbutas</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1921">1921</a>–<a href="/years/1994">1994</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Marija Birutė Alseikaitė-Gimbutienė</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Marija Gimbutas was a Lithuanian archaeologist and anthropologist known for her research into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neolithic">Neolithic</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze-age">Bronze Age</a> cultures of Old <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and for her <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kurgan">Kurgan</a> hypothesis, which located the Proto-Indo-European homeland in the Pontic Steppe. Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vilnius">Vilnius</a>, she fled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a> before the advancing Soviet army and moved to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, where she was professor at UCLA from 1963 to 1989 and directed excavations of Neolithic sites in southeastern Europe. Her Goddess books described a goddess-centred Old Europe supplanted by patriarchal Kurgan invaders; mainstream archaeology dismissed these later works, while three genetic studies in 2015 gave support to her Kurgan theory.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/marija-gimbutas.jpg" alt="Marija Gimbutas" width="330" height="298" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Monica Boirar · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Prof-Dr-Marija-Gimbutas-Copyright-Foto-Monica-Boirar-aka-Monica-Beurer.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Modern scholars, from Lubor Niederle and Aleksander Gieysztor to Ivanov, Toporov, <b>Marija Gimbutas</b> and Rybakov, have rebuilt the religion by comparison with other Indo-European traditions and from 19th- and 20th-century folklore, though some of these studies are contested.</blockquote><a href="/religion/slavic-paganism">Slavic paganism · Texts &amp; Teachers</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Marija Gimbutas</b> based her theory of a peaceful, goddess-centred Old Europe partly on these figurines; her conclusions were always controversial and are now rejected by many scholars.</blockquote><a href="/culture/cucuteni-trypillia-culture">Cucuteni–Trypillia culture · Beliefs &amp; Worldview</a></li><li><blockquote>In her Kurgan hypothesis <b>Marija Gimbutas</b> identified the Yamnaya with the late Proto-Indo-Europeans, and this hypothesis is widely accepted.</blockquote><a href="/culture/yamnaya-culture">Yamnaya culture · Legacy</a></li></ul></section>
